WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.836 --> 00:00:06.339
NARRATOR: In 1976, a
deadly illness erupted

00:00:06.406 --> 00:00:07.741
in a remote province of Zaire.

00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:10.477
[music playing]

00:00:10.543 --> 00:00:15.382
Belgian nuns tending to the
sick described horrific symptoms

00:00:15.448 --> 00:00:18.084
followed by agonizing deaths.

00:00:18.151 --> 00:00:20.120
REID WILSON: It attacks
tissue around the body.

00:00:20.186 --> 00:00:22.522
It basically attacks
everything in the human body

00:00:22.589 --> 00:00:24.224
except for your bones.

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:27.394
NARRATOR: Doctors from
Zaire, Europe, and America

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:30.530
traveled to the
epicenter to investigate.

00:00:30.597 --> 00:00:32.332
REID WILSON: When
responders got there,

00:00:32.399 --> 00:00:33.767
they had no idea what
they were looking at.

00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:35.335
But they did know
that they were looking

00:00:35.402 --> 00:00:37.804
at this deadly new virus.

00:00:37.871 --> 00:00:38.972
NARRATOR: The
researchers might not

00:00:39.038 --> 00:00:41.241
have known what
this new virus was,

00:00:41.307 --> 00:00:43.276
but they learned how
it spread so quickly.

00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:44.811
[whoosh]

00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:47.547
Medical supplies
were so scarce here

00:00:47.614 --> 00:00:49.282
that the Belgian
nurses had resorted

00:00:49.349 --> 00:00:51.317
to reusing hypodermic needles.

00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:53.520
[music playing]

00:00:53.586 --> 00:01:00.093
This virus had an astounding
mortality rate of nearly 90%.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:04.798
In less than two months,
it claimed some 300 lives.

00:01:04.864 --> 00:01:06.132
[whoosh]

00:01:06.199 --> 00:01:10.637
Then suddenly, the nightmare
ended, and no one knew why.

00:01:10.703 --> 00:01:13.473
NANCY JAAX: They just knew the
virus popped out of nowhere,

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:16.543
and it disappeared
just as quickly.

00:01:16.609 --> 00:01:19.379
NARRATOR: Epidemiologists
named Ebola Zaire

00:01:19.446 --> 00:01:24.651
after the nearby Ebola river
and dreaded its return,

00:01:24.717 --> 00:01:28.922
which it did in just a
few relatively contained

00:01:28.988 --> 00:01:30.356
outbreaks over the next decade.

00:01:33.126 --> 00:01:35.862
But the greatest fear was
that someday the virus

00:01:35.929 --> 00:01:37.864
would emerge in a
densely populated

00:01:37.931 --> 00:01:40.533
region, which it also did.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.469
[music playing]

00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:45.538
REID WILSON: So back in 1989,
in the Washington suburb

00:01:45.605 --> 00:01:48.675
of Reston, all of a sudden, a
bunch of monkeys at a warehouse

00:01:48.741 --> 00:01:50.743
started dying.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:52.545
NARRATOR: Hazleton
Laboratories, Reston,

00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:54.981
Virginia, one of the
largest animal testing

00:01:55.048 --> 00:01:57.584
companies in the country.

00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:59.686
A shipment of 100
long-tailed macaques

00:01:59.752 --> 00:02:02.388
came in from the Philippines.

00:02:02.455 --> 00:02:05.892
Two were dead on arrival.

00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:09.996
The illness quickly spread
to dozens of monkeys.

00:02:10.063 --> 00:02:12.665
Desperate for a
diagnosis, the company

00:02:12.732 --> 00:02:18.238
sent samples to a high security
lab run by the military, a US

00:02:18.304 --> 00:02:21.908
Army Medical Research Institute
of Infectious Diseases,

00:02:21.975 --> 00:02:23.409
or USAMRID.

00:02:23.476 --> 00:02:24.611
[whoosh]

00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:27.847
Lieutenant Colonel Nancy
Jaax examined the tissues.

00:02:27.914 --> 00:02:30.683
NANCY JAAX: What we found then
was really pretty alarming.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:33.720
I was seeing a section of liver.

00:02:33.786 --> 00:02:36.155
I saw all these
very large cells--

00:02:36.222 --> 00:02:37.991
which are called macrophages--

00:02:38.057 --> 00:02:41.594
and they were full
of material which

00:02:41.661 --> 00:02:44.264
is very typical in a
filovirus infection.

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:45.465
[whoosh]

00:02:45.532 --> 00:02:49.068
NARRATOR: A filovirus, a
tiny spaghetti-like strand

00:02:49.135 --> 00:02:51.538
that replicates
at lightning speed

00:02:51.604 --> 00:02:54.574
and causes devastating diseases.

00:02:54.641 --> 00:02:57.176
Dr. Jaax was very
familiar with them.

00:02:57.243 --> 00:03:00.113
She worked behind layers
of bomb resistant walls

00:03:00.179 --> 00:03:03.316
where the army stored an
archive of frozen killers.

00:03:03.383 --> 00:03:04.851
[whoosh] Marburg--

00:03:04.918 --> 00:03:05.852
[whoosh]

00:03:05.919 --> 00:03:06.853
--Ebola Sudan--

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:07.954
[whoosh]

00:03:08.021 --> 00:03:12.926
--and the most lethal
of all, Ebola Zaire.

00:03:12.992 --> 00:03:15.495
Dr. Jaax used her
stash of filoviruses

00:03:15.562 --> 00:03:19.165
to compare it to the monkey
sample, and to her surprise,

00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:20.934
the one that matched
was the virus

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.637
that struck Zaire in 1976.

00:03:24.704 --> 00:03:26.306
We're thinking, well,
that's not possible we

00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:27.907
need to check it again.

00:03:27.974 --> 00:03:30.543
And they checked it three
times, and every time,

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:31.611
there was no mix up.

00:03:31.678 --> 00:03:33.446
It was showing positive
for Ebola Zaire.

00:03:36.516 --> 00:03:38.251
JERRY JAAX: I think Nancy
came to my office and said--

00:03:38.318 --> 00:03:39.719
NANCY JAAX: I did
go to his office.

00:03:39.786 --> 00:03:41.754
JERRY JAAX: --you're not going
to believe what just happened.

00:03:41.821 --> 00:03:43.690
You know, it looks like
we've got Ebola virus

00:03:43.756 --> 00:03:45.792
in these monkeys in Virginia.

00:03:45.858 --> 00:03:48.661
There was a lot of confusion
and going, oh, wow.

00:03:48.728 --> 00:03:50.330
This is really crazy.

00:03:50.396 --> 00:03:51.631
What are we going to do?

00:03:51.698 --> 00:03:54.000
NANCY JAAX: We immediately
had to kick into high gear.

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:56.035
NARRATOR: The army
formed the battle plan.

00:03:56.102 --> 00:04:02.542
The first order, euthanize the
monkeys to prevent an epidemic.

00:04:02.609 --> 00:04:06.145
Nancy's husband, Colonel
Jerry Jaax, led the team.

00:04:06.212 --> 00:04:07.814
JERRY JAAX: Nobody
likes killing monkeys,

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:09.482
but we were in an
emergency situation.

00:04:09.549 --> 00:04:12.185
It was something that we
had to do to make sure

00:04:12.251 --> 00:04:13.753
that we didn't have a
significant outbreak

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:15.955
within the human population.

00:04:16.022 --> 00:04:17.824
NARRATOR: They dodged a bullet.

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:20.627
And they soon realized
this strain of Ebola

00:04:20.693 --> 00:04:23.896
wasn't harmful to
humans, but they

00:04:23.963 --> 00:04:25.632
knew it was only
a matter of time

00:04:25.698 --> 00:04:28.334
before they'd have to
face the unimaginable.

00:04:28.401 --> 00:04:29.535
NANCY JAAX: The
question, of course,

00:04:29.602 --> 00:04:31.104
is are you going
to get a mutation

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:36.376
that then makes it more airborne
or makes it more contagious.

00:04:36.442 --> 00:04:39.045
JERRY JAAX: What we did
in a contained building

00:04:39.112 --> 00:04:43.983
was pretty dramatic, but
imagine if it was a city.

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:45.184
REID WILSON: The
nightmare scenario

00:04:45.251 --> 00:04:46.719
for global public
health officials

00:04:46.786 --> 00:04:49.756
is something that combines the
transmissibility of something

00:04:49.822 --> 00:04:51.124
like the Spanish flu--

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:54.627
which infected probably about a
billion people a century ago--

00:04:54.694 --> 00:04:56.262
and the mortality of Ebola.

00:04:56.329 --> 00:04:57.630
NAHID BHADELIA: What
keeps me up at night

00:04:57.697 --> 00:05:00.833
is that we'll have a disease
that's transmitted by droplets.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:03.503
ANTHONY FAUCI: Something
that is respiratory-borne

00:05:03.569 --> 00:05:08.374
that is a virus like influenza,
likely influenza, that has

00:05:08.441 --> 00:05:11.778
both high degree of
transmissibility and high

00:05:11.844 --> 00:05:13.413
degree of morbidity
and mortality.

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:15.448
REID WILSON: If something like
that spreads around the world,

00:05:15.515 --> 00:05:16.949
we're just not
ready to fight it.

00:05:17.016 --> 00:05:17.850
[whoosh]

00:05:17.917 --> 00:05:18.918
[whoosh]

